irmation before proceeding. The programming should reflect the anomalous nature of these outlying cases.\n\n\nThe user's skepticism regarding the 1km tolerance is valid; the original methodology relies on strict topological intersection and does not include a buffer. Implementing a tolerance would introduce an unsupported deviation from the established approach. The code will treat any cases beyond strict intersection as anomalous, ensuring alignment with the original methodology and user's intent.\n\n\nThe initial 32 cases (22 with province/canton mismatch + 10 in former undefined zones) are addressed, increasing the geo-corrected count. The cases in the maritime/coastal zone do not intersect any terrestrial polygon. The remaining cases are in either the water, or on land borders of two cantons.\n\n\nThe 12 cases previously considered maritime are in fact on land, situated on terrestrial borders between cantons. These include cases between Huaquillas–Arenillas, Tulcán–San Pedro de Huaca, Tisaleo–Mocha, Juján–Yaguachi and Balzar–Mocache. The original project design handles these 30 cases (18 maritime/estuarine and 12 terrestrial) correctly as they fall into the \"limbo_formato_correcto\" group, which retains cases without altering original data. No artificial buffer is implemented.\n\n\nThe 32 cases requiring correction necessitate `Point-in-Polygon` checks against the shapefile data to determine accurate canton assignments. The architecture requires tracking where this check happens. `Fase 4` or `Fase 2` are the likely candidates, but confirmation depends on the data's need for the shapefile/bounds data for all 32 cases. For cases with identical province and canton names, their geographical coordinates or shapefile intersection are essential for accurate assignment to the correct canton. The next step is a code trace to determine the best place to introduce the point-in-polygon check.\n\n\n`Point-in-Polygon` checks confirm canton reassignments. The checks against the shapefile data indicate the 22 initial cases in `QUITO` are correctly assigned, while the four Orellana cases belong to La Joya de los Sachas.  The nine Ibarra cases within the Las Golondrinas polygon belong in Cotacachi, and the single case in the El Piedrero polygon in Guayas belongs to Cañar-La Troncal. The key functions for these operations are located within a specific module that has audit functions. Specifically, adding a clear function to this module ensures easy correction of jurisdiction by location.\n\n\nA new function, `point_in_polygon_reassignment`, will implement the `Point-in-Polygon` check, taking a GeoDataFrame as input. This function will be integrated either within `auditar_punto_en_poligono` or just before the spatial join operation within the same function. The console output will now explicitly report the 22 cases corrected by the Point-in-Polygon approach, the 10 jurisdiction cases and the 30 anomalous cases in the `limbo_formato_correcto` group, clearly distinguishing between the maritime/estuarine cases and the terrestrial inter-cantonal border cases within `separar_por_formato`. A quick test needs to be done on the current system, to verify it doesn't break.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"read_file","args":{"Path":"D:\\Tesis\\src\\validacion_geo\\geometrias.py"}}]}
{"step_index":500,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T22:03:08Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-10T17:03:08-05:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-10T17:03:08-05:00\nShowing lines 1-348 of 348\n1: \"\"\"\n2: geometrias.py — Creación de puntos geoespaciales y auditoría topológica.\n3: \n4: Corresponde a las Fases R 6-7 (líneas 232-298) del código original:\n5:     Fase 6: Conversión de coordenadas a puntos SF\n6:     Fase 7: Primera auditoría topológica punto-en-polígono\n7: \n8: Funciones principales:\n9:     - crear_geodataframe_puntos(): Convierte coords a GeoDataFrame (Paso 4.3)\n10:     - auditar_punto_en_poligono(): sjoin vectorizado (Paso 4.4)\n11:     - separar_por_formato(): Diagnóstico de limbo (Paso 4.5)\n12: \n13: En palabras simples:\n14:     1) Convierte los números de latitud/longitud de cada homicidio en un\n15:        \"punto en el mapa\".\n16:     2) Comprueba si cada punto cae DENTRO del cantón donde el parte policial\n17:        dice que ocurrió.\n18:     3) Los que caen fuera los divide en dos grupos: los que tienen punto\n19:        decimal (ej. -2.18, probablemente un error de límite cantonal) y los\n20:        que NO tienen punto decimal (ej. 21894, error de digitación que se\n21:        intentará reparar en reparacion.py).\n22: \"\"\"\n23: import geopandas as gpd  # Manejo de tablas con geometrías (puntos, polígonos).\n24: import numpy as np       # Librería numérica base sobre la que se apoyan pandas y geopandas.\n25: import pandas as pd      # Manejo de tablas normales (DataFrames).\n26: \n27: from src.ingesta.config import CRS_OBJETIVO  # 4326 (WGS84)\n28: \n29: \n30: # =========================================================================\n31: # PASO 4.3 — Creación de puntos geoespaciales\n32: # =========================================================================\n33: def crear_geodataframe_puntos(df: pd.DataFrame) -> gpd.GeoDataFrame:\n34:     \"\"\"\n35:     Convierte un DataFrame con columnas latitud/longitud a GeoDataFrame de puntos.\n36: \n37:     Equivalencia R (líneas 235-260):\n38:         homi_2010_2024_fin <- homi_2010_2024 %>%\n39:             mutate(lat.new = as.numeric(X21), lon.new = as.numeric(X22)) %>%\n40:             filter(!is.na(lat.new) & !is.na(lon.new))\n41:         homi_2010_2024_fin$ID.temp <- 1:nrow(homi_2010_2024_fin)\n42:         point_analisis <- data.frame(y = ...$lat.new, x = ...$lon.new)\n43:         point_analisis <- st_as_sf(point_analisis, coords = c(\"x\",\"y\"), crs = ...)\n44: \n45:     Mapeo R → Python:\n46:         as.numeric()              →  pd.to_numeric(errors='coerce')\n47:         filter(!is.na())          →  df.dropna(subset=[...])\n48:         1:nrow(df)                →  range(1, len(df)+1)\n49:         st_as_sf(coords=c(x,y))  →  gpd.GeoDataFrame(geometry=points_from_xy())\n50: \n51:     Parameters\n52:     ----------\n53:     df : pd.DataFrame\n54:         DataFrame filtrado de Fase 3 con columnas 'latitud' y 'longitud'\n55:         como strings.\n56: \n57:     Returns\n58:     -------\n59:     gpd.GeoDataFrame\n60:         GeoDataFrame con geometría de puntos (Point), columna 'ID_temp'\n61:         secuencial, y columnas numéricas 'lat_num' / 'lon_num'.\n62: \n63:     Raises\n64:     ------\n65:     KeyError\n66:         Si las columnas 'latitud' o 'longitud' no existen.\n67:     \"\"\"\n68:     # (\"latitud\", \"longitud\") es una TUPLA: como una lista, pero inmutable\n69:     # (no se puede modificar). Verificamos que ambas columnas existan.\n70:     for col in (\"latitud\", \"longitud\"):\n71:         if col not in df.columns:\n72:             raise KeyError(\n73:                 f\"La columna '{col}' no existe en el DataFrame. \"\n74:                 f\"Columnas disponibles: {list(df.columns)}\"\n75:             )\n76: \n77:     print(\"  [4.3] Creación de puntos geoespaciales\")\n78: \n79:     df = df.copy()  # Trabajamos sobre una copia para no alterar el df original.\n80: \n81:     # Preservar las cadenas originales para el algoritmo reparador (Paso 4.6)\n82:     # Estas columnas serán necesarias para diagnosticar el formato decimal\n83:     #\n84:     # ¿Por qué guardar el texto original ANTES de convertir a número?\n85:     # Porque al convertir \"-4e-04\" o \"21894\" a número decimal (float), Python\n86:     # le cambia la representación (ej. 21894 → 21894.0 ¡le inventa un punto!).\n87:     # Si no guardamos el text